#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147176 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7328749-7ecf-5040-9d18-e9274187d485" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Time"}] :question/id #uuid "94d6edd2-52f9-581a-84f0-3003ccbaadd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14266" :question/problem "The number of hedgehogs $N(t)$ in a particular nature reserve after $t$ years is modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = 30 \\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nHow many years will it take for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the time it takes for the number of hedgehogs to reach $7\\,680,$ we need to solve the equation $N(t)=7\\,680$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =7\\,680 \\\\ 30\\cdot {2}^{t} & =7\\,680 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{7\\,680}{30} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =256 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $t=8$ years for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147177 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "363026fc-b4ac-5b35-8825-717a113df12b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Model and Solving for an Unknown Time"}] :question/id #uuid "4a2c8602-04d5-5ec7-846f-2647249994fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14267" :question/problem "Bob invests $\\[math]250$ into some high-growth stocks, and the value of his investment doubles every year. How long will it take for Bob's investment to increase to $\\[/math]16\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "We know that Bob's investment doubles every year. So the value of Bob's investment, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $2.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=250$ is the initial investment (in dollars).\n\n- $b=2$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the investment was first made.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 250\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time taken for the investment to reach $\\[math]16\\,000,$  we need to solve the equation $y(t) = 16\\,000$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =16\\,000 \\\\ 250\\cdot {2}^{t} & =16\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{16\\,000}{250} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =64 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $6$ years for Bob's investment to grow to $\\[/math]16\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147178 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147423 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08d13ef7-5569-5cc4-9692-9ed99a3be3e8" :knowledge-point/title "Creating and Solving a Model Given Some Percentage Growth"}] :question/id #uuid "9759efbf-fdb7-595c-822d-85c6b5f3549e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14271" :question/problem "Mary is conducting a scientific experiment with bacteria. She notices that the number of bacteria in a Petri dish grows by $15\\%$ each day. If there were initially $100$ bacteria in the dish, what equation does Mary need to solve to predict how long it will take for the number of bacteria to increase to $535?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $15\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=100$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $r = 0.15$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the experiment is started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 100\\cdot(1+0.15)^t = 100\\cdot (1.15)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the number of bacteria to reach $535,$ we would need to solve the equation $y(t)= 535,$ which gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\cdot (1.15)^{t} & =535.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147179 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147424 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c127787-8a31-524b-8b7d-68c15b714335" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Initial Value"}] :question/id #uuid "07a30fba-ad4b-56a0-b6ce-21941d106b38" :question/math-academy-id "e-14276" :question/problem "Some scientists are monitoring the growth of the wolf population in a particular nature reserve. The population of wolves $W(t)$ after $t$ decades of observations is given by\n\n$$\nW(t)  = W_{0} \\cdot 4^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $W_{0}$ represents the wolves' population at the beginning of the study. If there were $3\\,200$ wolves after $3$ decades, how many wolves were there at the beginning of the study?" :question/worked-solution "There are $3\\,200$  wolves  $3$ decades after the scientists started studying the wolf population, so $W(3)  = 3\\,200.$\n\nTo find the initial number of wolves $W_0,$ we substitute $t=3$ and $W(3)  = 3\\,200$ into the given equation and solve for $W_0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & ={W}_{0}\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 3\\,200 & ={W}_{0}\\cdot 64 \\\\ {W}_{0} & =\\frac{3\\,200}{64} \\\\ {W}_{0} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $50$ wolves at the beginning of the study."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7328749-7ecf-5040-9d18-e9274187d485" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8569f2c3-47f2-55dc-af22-e693e573aabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496932} {:answer/id #uuid "be6d85cd-dc8f-54f0-bb5e-4be03a2c345a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497046} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bdd980-4b02-5c9d-87c0-3ae5bf423ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497100} {:answer/id #uuid "effec5c8-d58c-5634-8d36-a27334f6e96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497175} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f74bde-3c91-5669-b932-9a4c8c5d4ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "effec5c8-d58c-5634-8d36-a27334f6e96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497175} :answer-field/id #uuid "713da8a1-7660-5675-94ac-ca37f7a2d59d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f3a7c31-c6fb-510a-b749-2c21cfbaae09" :question/math-academy-id "q-112257" :question/problem "A social media influencer had $100$ followers the day she created her account. Since then, the number $F(t)$ of followers after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nF(t)=100\\cdot {3}^{t}.\n$$\n\nHow many weeks will it take for the followers to increase to $72\\,900?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the time it takes for the number of followers to increase to $72\\,900,$ we need to solve the equation $F(t)=72\\,900$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =72\\,900 \\\\ 100\\cdot {3}^{t} & =72\\,900 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =\\frac{72\\,900}{100} \\\\ {3}^{t} & =729 \\\\ {3}^{t} & ={3}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of followers increases to $72\\,900$ after $6$ weeks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147423 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08d13ef7-5569-5cc4-9692-9ed99a3be3e8" :knowledge-point/title "Creating and Solving a Model Given Some Percentage Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84866a2b-bc2b-526b-8d03-f790bfc3078a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot (1.3)^{t}=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496929} {:answer/id #uuid "942ab900-0884-5729-97ce-f1a6fbeb9244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot {3}^{t}=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496965} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc19a1f-7ee5-5af0-81fb-257d6f3a4cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot (1.03)^{t}=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497050} {:answer/id #uuid "c73780c0-4767-5594-b17d-a5c24ba647cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\cdot 1.3)^{t}=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497069} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fe32b5-a478-58d5-959e-0ea2936100a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot (0.3)^{t}=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84866a2b-bc2b-526b-8d03-f790bfc3078a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot (1.3)^{t}=100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496929} :answer-field/id #uuid "29893dcd-fc6d-57fa-af02-82a279b31b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25c45b73-9502-506c-8f02-7a944a17847b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112309" :question/problem "A fungi culture increases by $30%$ each week. If the culture started with $5$ fungi, what equation should we solve to determine how many weeks will it take for the fungi culture to increase to $100?$" :question/worked-solution "The fungi culture $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $30%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of fungi.\n\n- $r=0.3$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the culture started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\cdot (1+0.3)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (1.3)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the number of weeks it takes for the fungi culture to increase to $100,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =100 \\\\ 5\\cdot (1.3)^{t} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147424 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c127787-8a31-524b-8b7d-68c15b714335" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Initial Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123a1ad9-baf5-5e6e-bac2-3666b9f37fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496697} {:answer/id #uuid "176f851e-2ff0-5aa9-94f6-f174585b3bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496708} {:answer/id #uuid "34be7b39-3787-56c4-a8a1-cc4816e6c5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496772} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9470ef-ff15-5d7b-bfe8-1989c9744591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496828} {:answer/id #uuid "d028f0b1-d088-54a7-9ec8-02a5463da751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "123a1ad9-baf5-5e6e-bac2-3666b9f37fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496697} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa37ed2-9413-56be-8870-8d9839afae71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e669fba-a4ac-508f-acb9-2cbafeb51763" :question/math-academy-id "q-112315" :question/problem "In a certain country, the number of people $P(t)$ who know a second language, $t$ years after $2005,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the number of people who knew a second language in $2005.$ If in $2010$ the number of people who knew a second language was $4\\,860\\,000,$ how many people knew a second language in $2005?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of people who knew a second language in $2010$ was $4\\,860\\,000,$ this is $5$ years after $2005.$ Thus, $P(5)=4\\,860\\,000.$\n\nTo find ${P}_{0},$ the number of people who knew a second lenguage in $2005,$  we substitute $t=5$ and $P(5)=4\\,860\\,000$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 4\\,860\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{4\\,860\\,000}{243} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =20\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in $2005$ there were $20\\,000$ people who knew a second language in that country."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147424 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c127787-8a31-524b-8b7d-68c15b714335" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Initial Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6ae88e-4e51-540f-8e0d-0e267fff6e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$11\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496718} {:answer/id #uuid "4054edd0-bb58-5d0d-8e3f-29eefdeffe97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$10\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496797} {:answer/id #uuid "6c95ca80-b0c5-5fe3-901a-fbd6f43e7164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496892} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c9d171-29f0-595c-94c3-48740f780d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497003} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4814a9-6d0e-5452-8762-e165bb579db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$40\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea4814a9-6d0e-5452-8762-e165bb579db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$40\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497160} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb1457d4-2953-5025-b958-130faff94c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cbabf22-40c3-5c95-8c42-a48a71281e71" :question/math-academy-id "q-112316" :question/problem "The profits of a store $P(t),$ $t$ years after $2018,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the profit in $2018.$ If the profits in $2020$ were of $$48\\,400,$ what was the profit in $2018?$" :question/worked-solution "The store profits in $2020$ were of $$48\\,400,$ this is $2$ years after $2018.$ Thus, $P(2)=48\\,400.$\n\nTo find the profit ${P}_{0}$ in $2018,$  we substitute $t=2$ and $P(2)=48\\,400$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ 48\\,400 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{48\\,400}{1.21} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =40\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profits in $2018$ were of $$40\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "363026fc-b4ac-5b35-8825-717a113df12b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Model and Solving for an Unknown Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b86eb9f-4d78-54f3-9bcc-45d5fe13a16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b86eb9f-4d78-54f3-9bcc-45d5fe13a16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496827} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e06da05-5e62-506a-b486-be82db39219e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94ed1bcc-bdbb-5a68-affe-a9e769dd8927" :question/math-academy-id "q-237311" :question/problem "The population of ducks living in a nature reserve triples every year. If there were $8$ ducks initially, how many years will it take for the population to increase to $5\\,832?$\n\nThe population of ducks increases to $5\\,832$ after{{field-1}}years." :question/worked-solution "We know that the duck population triples every year. Thus, the duck population, which we will call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $3.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=8$ is the initial population of ducks.\n\n- $b=3$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=8\\cdot {3}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the population of ducks to increase to $5\\,832,$ we need to solve the equation $y(t)=5\\,832$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\,832 \\\\ 8\\cdot {3}^{t} & =5\\,832 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =\\frac{5\\,832}{8} \\\\ {3}^{t} & =729 \\\\ {3}^{t} & ={3}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population of ducks increases to $5\\,832$ after $6$ years."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147423 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08d13ef7-5569-5cc4-9692-9ed99a3be3e8" :knowledge-point/title "Creating and Solving a Model Given Some Percentage Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "470a32ac-1f39-5cd0-9165-8909dac7971c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "470a32ac-1f39-5cd0-9165-8909dac7971c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496814} :answer-field/id #uuid "48fde31c-ad75-5542-a64f-929ac3239408" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f316582c-e7bf-5ff9-8e9a-83d59a1c3910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f316582c-e7bf-5ff9-8e9a-83d59a1c3910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497179} :answer-field/id #uuid "beff01d7-6cb0-54e0-bc71-2ce1a1269b34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65185e0f-08b4-53fe-970a-61a3c4a92c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-237332" :question/problem "Paul is conducting a scientific experiment with amoebas. He notices that the number of amoebas grows by  $65%$ each day. If there were initially $140$ amoebas, complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how long it will take for the number of amoebas to increase to $800.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,(${{field-2}}$)^{t}=800$" :question/worked-solution "The amoeba population $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $65%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=140$ is the initial number of amoebas.\n\n- $r=0.65$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =140\\cdot (1+0.65)^{t} \\\\  & =140\\cdot (1.65)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the amoeba population to increase to $800,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =800 \\\\ 140\\cdot (1.65)^{t} & =800.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "363026fc-b4ac-5b35-8825-717a113df12b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Model and Solving for an Unknown Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d61264-f40f-5d03-8c04-a35fe010f7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496659} {:answer/id #uuid "d79c959b-480e-5606-b96e-1a1b0caa28f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497112} {:answer/id #uuid "d9095599-b7ed-56a3-a93d-492035719742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497116} {:answer/id #uuid "e19e08c9-87f0-537a-96a1-72e7dd131284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497141} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcaf9ba-8932-5825-8a62-1358baee383a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00d61264-f40f-5d03-8c04-a35fe010f7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496659} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e9c3a9-039d-5389-8d3a-67841500f704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74b32b7f-8020-5b1d-8650-2271e11205ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-45649" :question/problem "An economic study in Chile showed that its gross domestic product (GDP) increases fourfold each decade. If the GDP was $$0.5$ billion in $2010$, how many decades will it take for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion?" :question/worked-solution "We know that the GDP increases fourfold each decade. So the GDP, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=0.5$ billion dollars was the GDP in $2010.$\n\n- $b=4$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in decades, since $2010.$\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=0.5\\cdot {4}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion, we need to solve the equation $y(t)=512$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =512 \\\\ 0.5\\cdot {4}^{t} & =512 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =\\frac{512}{0.5} \\\\ {4}^{t} & =1\\,024 \\\\ {4}^{t} & ={4}^{5} \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it will take $5$ decades for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7328749-7ecf-5040-9d18-e9274187d485" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d474c02-8953-5638-9006-799781937e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496791} {:answer/id #uuid "6b71812c-5e4b-5153-9260-0e1084106734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496890} {:answer/id #uuid "77717bbb-4108-5347-b237-95ef0dfb283c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496908} {:answer/id #uuid "c63d0890-0e3b-5478-b9f5-110e856e0845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497064} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8a0689-c144-5e95-8b08-03494f30d0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8a0689-c144-5e95-8b08-03494f30d0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497173} :answer-field/id #uuid "0373459d-6a90-5e83-a06b-57aa4a31bdbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "519a949e-8b2c-53c0-bd01-456c0a3f29d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-61756" :question/problem "The number $N(t)$ of bees in a hive after $t$ months can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=5\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t}.\n$$\n\nHow many months will it take for the population of bees to increase to $40\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the time it takes for the number of bees to reach $40\\,000,$ we need to solve the equation $N(t)=40\\,000$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =40\\,000 \\\\ 5\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t} & =40\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{40\\,000}{5\\,000} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of bees increases to $40\\,000$ after $3$ months."}]}
